# Experiments for the "opening Neovim kills the whole tmux server" fault.
#
# The fault is intermittent and only appears with a real Windows Terminal
# client attached over SSH, which is why it cannot be reproduced on the machine
# itself. Rather than guess, run one experiment at a time for long enough to be
# convincing, then move to the next.
#
# Copy this file to 00-crash-experiments.conf in the same directory, uncomment
# one block, and restart the tmux server. The local tier is never committed, so
# these stay on this machine.
#
# Keep notes. "It did not crash today" is only evidence if it usually would
# have by now.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Experiment 1: extended keys
#
# Best fit for the symptom. Neovim negotiates the terminal's extended key
# protocols while starting, and only while starting. It is the one thing it
# does that Claude, btop and a shell do not, which matches the fault appearing
# only at Neovim startup and never once Neovim is up. This machine's tmux has
# extended-keys on, and the .tmux fork carries upstream fixes (#804, #805)
# about extended-key detection misbehaving on terminals that mishandle it.
#
# set -g extended-keys off

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Experiment 2: no passthrough, no clipboard writes
#
# Both send data outward to the terminal and take replies back. Turning them
# off costs OSC 52 copying, so only run this one if experiment 1 comes back
# clean and the crashes continue.
#
# set -g allow-passthrough off
# set -s set-clipboard off

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Experiment 3: a newer tmux
#
# Ubuntu ships tmux 3.4, released in 2023, and the archive has nothing newer.
# tmux 3.7c installs without root:
#
#   mise use -g tmux@3.7c
#
# Two things to know before switching. The 3.4 build here has sixel support
# compiled in and the 3.7c build does not, so if the fault involves image data
# reaching tmux, upgrading removes that code path entirely. And the gpakosz
# configuration in tmux-omt does not load under 3.7c at all, so the submodule
# has to be updated to a version that supports it first. Check with:
#
#   tmux show -gv @plugin     # 'invalid option' means the config did not load
#
# There is no tmux setting for this experiment. It is a different binary.
